payload.core_explanation_md:
**Structure, top to bottom:** **Board** → **Audit Committee of the Board (ACB)** → **MD or executive director** → an **independent Compliance department** headed by the **CCO** → compliance staff at the **corporate office verticals** → **field offices** (**Field General Manager's offices** where they exist, **regional**, **zonal** offices, **branches**).

**Who reports to whom:**

- the **CCO** reports directly to the **MD and CEO** and/or the **Board or ACB**; if to the MD and CEO, the **ACB meets the CCO quarterly one-to-one**, without senior management;
- **business and functional departments** report to the **CCO** on compliance;
- **Compliance Officers at regional and zonal offices** report to the **CCO** directly;
- the CCO **reviews the performance of every Compliance Officer**; the **Board or ACB** reviews the **CCO's** appraisal.

**Choosing the CCO:** a **senior-level selection committee** set up by the **Board** recommends names **in order of merit**; the **Board decides**. **RBI's Department of Supervision, Central Office** is told **beforehand** of an appointment, premature transfer or removal, with the **profile**, the **fit and proper certificate** by the **MD and CEO** and the **reasons** for any change.

**With the compliance staff, senior management must:**

1. **apprise** the Board and senior management of **rules and developments**;
2. **clarify** compliance questions;
3. **assess compliance risk at least yearly** and plan activity; the **plan goes to the ACB** and is **available to internal audit**;
4. **report promptly** on major changes;
5. **report failures and breaches** periodically to the Board or ACB and circulate to functional heads;
6. **test compliance** with representative testing and report the results;
7. check **sustenance** of compliance;
8. ensure **RBI's supervisory observations** are met **in letter and spirit, on time and sustainably**;
9. (courseware) identify the **main compliance risks** each year and plan to manage them.

Held at needs_review (courseware). Main functions:

- **identify** which business functions each **guideline** touches and **assess risk** by line, product and process;
- **pass on** regulator and **Ministry of Finance** instructions promptly and get **conclusive compliance** reported to the **ACB quarterly**;
- sit on the **Product Group Committee** to **vet new products** from the compliance angle and **monitor new products intensively for six months**;
- give **inspectors and concurrent auditors a checklist**; **test compliance** on a **sample basis in selected zones each quarter**, reporting to the **MD and CEO**;
- coordinate with **RBI's Senior Supervisory Manager team** in **risk based supervision** (data for **Tranches I, IA, IB, II and III**, reviewed at **DGM or AGM level**);
- coordinate **returns and replies** to RBI and guide on **cross-border** business.

The **CCO is the nodal contact with RBI**. Directions from **IRDA, SEBI and others** go to **separate compliance officers**; any **discomfort** voiced by another regulator is **taken to RBI through the CCO**.

Held at needs_review (courseware).

Head office **functional departments** run **their own controls** and **report breaches promptly** to the Compliance department. Each **functional head designates a senior officer (minimum scale IV or V)** as the department's **compliance officer**, who:

- **lists compliance issues** from guidelines and sends them to the Compliance department;
- **acts on** issues the department flags and **monitors and reports**;
- keeps **registers of returns**, ensures **timely regulatory returns** and that **agendas go to the Board, ACB and the Supervisory Committee on Risk**;
- turns guidelines into **actionable instructions** (technology flags, exception reports, job cards, manuals, training).

**The department also:** builds **compliance culture**; **vets the quality and integrity** of data going to RBI; holds **prime responsibility** for monitoring compliance in its area; **updates manuals and codes of conduct** (on the bank's website); forwards **RBI and MOF communications** for action; and sends a **quarterly compliance certificate** to the Compliance department. The Compliance department gives the **ACB a quarterly note** confirming compliance with RBI and MOF circulars.

Held at needs_review (courseware).

**Branch:** the **front line unit** of the compliance hierarchy. **Conflict of interest** between managing compliance risk and business growth cannot be fully avoided, so it must be **kept from getting in the way**. The **branch manager, accountant and officers** are primarily responsible; the **branch manager is also the branch's compliance officer**.

**FGMO, zonal and regional offices:** **compliance officers** are designated there and **report directly to the CCO** and apprise the local head of **compliance levels and breaches**. These offices:

1. report to the **central compliance department**;
2. send a **quarterly status** of the whole command area in a prescribed format;
3. **report and escalate breaches immediately**;
4. have the **Internal Control and Inspection department** cover compliance in **inspections**;
5. act in **handholding, trouble shooting and monitoring** for branches.

Held at needs_review (courseware).

Each **centre covering a cluster of foreign branches or representative offices** has an **independent Compliance department**:

- headed by a **local compliance officer**, preferably or together with an **India-based officer in Scale III or IV**, supported by local staff;
- the centre's compliance officer has a **dotted line to the CCO**, monitors **all branches in the centre**, sends **monthly reports**, and does **compliance testing (preferably quarterly)** reported independently to the CCO;
- the **chief executive of the centre** implements the **bank's policy or the local guidelines, whichever is more stringent**;
- a **dedicated overseas compliance desk at head office** gives oversight and ensures timely reporting to **top management, ACB, Board and RBI**.

Held as the courseware's account (Basel Committee framework).

**Objectives:** efficient and effective operations; accurate records; reliable financial reporting; effective risk management systems; compliance with laws, regulations and internal policies. Controls remain exposed to **execution risk** because people run them.

**Typical controls:** **limit authorities**; **safeguard access** to records and assets; **separate and rotate duties**; **regular and unscheduled reviews** including testing.

**Five components:**

1. **Control environment** (structure, management philosophy, integrity and competence, external influences, Board and committee attention, HR policies);
2. **Risk assessment**;
3. **Control activities**;
4. **Accounting, information and communication systems**;
5. **Self-assessment or monitoring**.

Held at needs_review (courseware).

**Culture:** compliance goes **beyond what is legally binding** to **integrity and ethics**, shaped by **tone from the top, incentive structure, accountability, communication, transparency, awareness and controls**.

**Five customer rights compliance protects:** **Fair Treatment**; **Transparency, Fair and Honest Dealing**; **Suitability**; **Privacy**; **Grievance Redress and Compensation**.

**RBI's expectations (risk based supervision):**

- **business, risk management, compliance and internal audit** are the **three lines of defence**;
- the **Board** puts **policies** in place documenting the **purpose, authority and responsibility** of the oversight functions;
- they are **independent** of business with a **co-operative** relationship, have the **same stature**, enough **experienced and qualified staff**;
- the Board keeps a **quality assurance and improvement programme** with **external assessment**;
- **senior management must not interfere** with their independence;
- **compliance is a core activity and must not be outsourced**.

